WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.920
A huge part of a hit reality show's appeal
comes from its colorful cast of real-life

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.920
characters who bounce off one another with
a unique chemistry any network sitcom would

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:09.900
go to war for.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.520
That's definitely the case with Storage Wars.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.400
What's the original cast of up to today?

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.160
Husband and wife team Dan and Laura Dotson
were the main auctioneers for the entire run

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:21.300
of Storage Wars.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:26.180
Their auction style was marked by classic
fast-talking patter, with the occasional reality-TV-friendly

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.160
squabble with a bidder.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.280
"Alright!

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:28.940
And if you're ready to go, I'm ready to go.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:29.440
How much money?

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:29.940
What's your pleasure?

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.480
Hey ma'am whatchya thinkin', how much?"

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.040
Now, Dan and Laura run their own private auctioneering
company, called American Auctioneers.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.480
They offer up auctions to the general public
throughout California, most of which are typical

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.340
legal sales and seized lockers.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.769
The couple also runs StorageAuctions.net,
which acts as an online database and facilitator

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:50.140
for storage facility auctions across the country.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.880
As The Blast reported, the Dotsons discovered
that they had sold a very special locker to

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.180
a client for $500 in 2018.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.360
What was inside?

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.700
Well, $7.5 million in cash.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.140
Dave Hester is the closest thing the show
had to an outright villain.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.120
He got into fights with his fellow bidders
and even the impartial auctioneers.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.979
He raised the prices on lockers he had no
intention of buying.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:17.000
And he did it all with his infectiously aggravating,
legally trademarked:

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.520
"YUUUP!"

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.600
According to USA Today, Hester tried to blow
the lid off the whole operation in 2012 when

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.030
he complained to A&amp;E producers that the program
was largely fake.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:32.660
When A&amp;E fired him, he filed a wrongful termination
suit for $750,000 in damages.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.720
However, the suit was settled in 2014, and
Hester returned to the show for the remainder

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:37.360
of its run.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:38.660
"Now shut up."

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.680
"Don't tell me to shut up or I'll tell you
to get the f--- out of my auction, how's that?"

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.700
Now, he runs an independent business as an
auctioneer and consultant.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.380
In an interview with SpareFoot, he said that
his eventual goal would be:

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.749
"To buy a motorhome, and not even have a home
address anymore, and just travel from one

00:01:53.749 --> 00:01:56.180
town to the next and be a gypsy auctioneer."

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.360
Known as "The Gambler," Darrell Sheets made
big swings on Storage Wars, often scoring

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:02.860
big.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:08.380
He once bought a locker for $3,600, only to
discover it contained artwork worth $300,000.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.060
"Hold on just a minute...Three hundred thousand
dollars?"

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.540
This was far and away the biggest profit made
on the entire show's run.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.560
Now, Sheets has somewhat retired from public
life and clearly enjoys being a family man

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.360
alongside his daughter and his fiancee.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.080
He's also undergone a dramatic weight loss,
shedding approximately 40 pounds using the

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.100
Nutrisystem method.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:34.080
Sadly, in March 2019, Sheets revealed on social
media that he was recovering from two back

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.850
to back heart attacks after years of being,
in his words,

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:41.700
"A closet diabetic that would eat whatever
[he] wanted and masked it with diabetic pills."

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.620
Let's hope for a safe and speedy recovery
for "The Gambler."

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.720
On Storage Wars, collector Barry Weiss disarmed
everyone with his unorthodox bidding style

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.760
and rat-a-tat roasts against his fellow storage
warriors.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.730
He impressed A&amp;E so much that in 2014, he
left the original show and was given his own

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:00.380
spinoff, Barry'd Treasure.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.680
On the show, which lasted for eight episodes,
he traveled across the country in search of

00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:08.819
hidden goodies and knick-knacks, showing off
his knowledge of classic cars in the process.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:13.680
The following year, he was given yet another
A&amp;E spinoff, called Storage Wars: Barry Strikes

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.180
Back.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:18.800
Weiss guided viewers through his favorite
Storage Wars episodes and moments, offering

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.760
behind-the-scenes tidbits along the way.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.760
"Oh here's where they're taking the potty
mouth."

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.120
Currently, Weiss has stepped away somewhat
from public life, he hasn't tweeted since

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:28.820
2014.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.180
In a couple of more recent TMZ interviews,
Weiss said he won't be appearing on TV anytime

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.900
soon, content with living life as, in his
words, a "professional slacker."

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.980
Mary's Storage Wars journey began on a spin-off.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.400
After her stint on Storage Wars: Texas, Mary
was brought on board the main ship in season

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:45.900
five.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.160
She earned her nickname "The Junkster" based
on her devil-may-care attitude toward finding

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:50.680
treasures.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.320
"Today this bull is a cash cow."

00:03:55.940 --> 00:04:00.620
Currently, Mary maintains a vintage store
called Mary's Finds, which sells, as she describes

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:05.120
it, "an eclectic mix of found treasures refurbished
or untouched," many of which were featured

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.420
in her personal Storage Wars journeys.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.959
According to her website, Mary has also worked
extensively with Ubuntu Life, an organization

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.600
dedicated to helping children with special
needs in Kenya.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.060
Mary personally visited Kenya, met with many
of the locals, and collected treasures along

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:19.560
the way.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.800
She sells some of her finds on her website,
with 20% of the proceeds going directly to

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:24.640
Ubuntu Life.

